                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/monoidal+categories+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#proof_structures"} Proof structures
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#proof_nets"} Proof nets
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#NetDeductions"} Nets of sequent deductions
              %li
                %a{:href => "#graphical_criterion_for_validity"} Graphical criterion for validity
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#description"} Description
              %li
                %a{:href => "#unitextended_proof_nets"} Unit-extended proof nets
              %li
                %a{:href => "#comparison_to_string_diagrams"} Comparison to string diagrams
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Proof nets
            are a graphical way to denote
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proofs"} proofs
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
          %p
            They were introduced by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Yves+Girard"} J.-Y. Girard
            in 1987 in order to remove spurious notational redundancies introduced by sequential rule application or indeterminacy of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cut+elimination"} cut elimination
            and can be viewed as graphical normal forms of proofs freed from ‘the bureaucracy of syntax’ (Girard).
          %p
            Bearing in mind the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            of (multiplicative intuitionistic)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            in (closed)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kelly-Mac+Lane+graphs"} Kelly-Mac Lane graphs
            that are used to track compositions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extranatural+transformations"} extranatural transformations
            definable in such categorical structures. More generally they may be compared to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
            that are evaluated in these categories (with various ways of making the comparison more precise). Proof nets for full linear logic have been thus described by
            %a{:href => "#Mellies06"} Melliès 06
            as string diagrams equipped with “boxes” to account for the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+modality"} exponential modality
          %p
            With
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
            interpreted as
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuits"} quantum circuits
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            (
            %a{:href => "#BlutePanangaden"}> Blute-Panangaden
            ).
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            We begin by describing proof nets for multiplicative linear logic (MLL), which is interpretable in
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+category"} autonomous
            categories. Similar proof nets may be given for other forms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
            such as multiplicative intuitionistic linear logic, which is interpretable in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+categories"} closed monoidal categories
          %h3#proof_structures Proof structures
          %p
            Before describing proof nets, we start with Girard’s notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %em proof structure
            = succeed "-free" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cut+rule"} cut
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kelly-Mac+Lane+graph"} Kelly-Mac Lane graph
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formula"} Formulas
            in MLL may be built from an alphabet
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{T}
            of propositional variables and two constants
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            by applying operations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊸
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \multimap
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi A
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊸
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg A \coloneqq A \multimap \bot
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⅋
                    %mi B
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊸
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \parr B \coloneqq \neg A \multimap B
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/planar+tree"} planar tree
          %p
            We consider two-sided
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequents"} sequents
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi m
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma = A_1, \ldots, A_m \vdash \Delta = B_1, \ldots, B_n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            to the left of the turnstyle are called
            %em negative
            and the formulas of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            are called
            = succeed "." do
              %em positive
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The subformulas
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S, T
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \otimes T
                have the same sign as
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \otimes T
            %li
              %p
                The subformula
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo ⊸
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \multimap T
                has the same sign as
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                        %mo ⊸
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \multimap T
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                has the opposite sign.
          %p
            We indicate the sign of a subformula using a superscript, e.g.,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^+
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A (cut-free)
              %em proof structure
              of type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo →
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \to \Delta
              (in the language of MLL) is a directed graph whose vertices are subformulas of the formulas of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma, \Delta
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  “KM-links” (Kelly-Mac Lane links) which are of the form
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi t
                          %mo −
                        %mo →
                        %msup
                          %mi t
                          %mo +
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t^- \to t^+
                  between two subformula occurrences of the same variable
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo ∈
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \in \mathbf{T}
                  but with opposite sign (always oriented from a negative occurrence to a positive occurrence);
              %li
                %p
                  Edges in binary construction trees of the formulas
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mi i
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mi j
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_i, B_j
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi S
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi T
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo −
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mo +
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi T
                              %mo +
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi S
                            %mo ⊸
                            %mi T
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo −
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mo −
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi T
                              %mo +
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                            %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                            %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↖
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mo −
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi T
                              %mo −
                            %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi S
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi T
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo +
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mo +
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi T
                              %mo −
                            %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi S
                            %mo ⊸
                            %mi T
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp; &amp; (S \otimes T)^- &amp; &amp; \qquad S^+ &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; T^+ &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; (S \multimap T)^- &amp; &amp; \qquad S^- &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; T^+ \\
                    \&amp; \swarrow &amp; &amp; \searrow &amp; \qquad &amp; \searrow &amp; &amp; \swarrow &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; \nearrow &amp; &amp; \searrow &amp; \qquad &amp; \nwarrow &amp; &amp; \swarrow &amp; \\
                    S^- &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; T^- \qquad &amp; &amp; (S \otimes T)^+ &amp; &amp; &amp; S^+ &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; T^- \qquad &amp; &amp; (S \multimap T)^+
                    }
          %p
            For a proof structure of type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo →
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \to \Delta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            are the KM-links. These KM-links give a KM-graph.
          %p
            MLL formulas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and proof structures of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B
            form a category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Struct
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Struct[\mathbf{T}]
          %p
            In fact this category of proof structures is a
            = succeed "-autonomous" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊸
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \multimap
            are defined on objects.
          %h3#proof_nets Proof nets
          %p
            Proof nets are those proof structures that arise by taking the KM-graphs of morphisms that are definable in the language of
            = succeed "-autonomous" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
          %p
            More exactly, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F[\mathbf{T}]
            be the free
            = succeed "-autonomous" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{T}
            (as discrete category), viewing
            = succeed "-autonomous" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            = succeed "-autonomous" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %em strictly
            as a 1-category that is 1-monadic over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F[\mathbf{T}]
            may be identified with MLL formulas. We view the morphisms of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F[\mathbf{T}]
            as representing morphisms that are definable (starting with the datum
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{T}
          %p
            As
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Struct
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Struct[\mathbf{T}]
            is
            = succeed "-autonomous," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi T
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi Struct
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{T} \hookrightarrow Struct[\mathbf{T}]
            induces a unique strict
            = succeed "-autonomous" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo :
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi Struct
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S: F[\mathbf{T}] \to Struct[\mathbf{T}]
            %em graphical semantics
            functor.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong proof net
              (in the language of MLL) of type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B
              is a proof structure
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
              of the form
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(f)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: A \to B
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F[\mathbf{T}]
          %p
            The following theorem for MLL proof nets, proved by Richard Blute in his thesis, is similar in content to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+theorem"} coherence theorem
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+categories"} closed monoidal categories
            proved by Kelly-Mac Lane.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              The restriction of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo :
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi Struct
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S: F[\mathbf{T}] \to Struct[\mathbf{T}]
              to the full subcategory consisting of objects isomorphic to formulas with no subformula occurrences of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
              or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
              is faithful.
          %p
            For a cedent of formulas
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma = A_1, \ldots, A_n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⨂
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigotimes \Gamma
            be the formula
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi …
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_1 \otimes \ldots \otimes A_n
            (associated to the left, say, if one is to be persnickety), and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⅋
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \parr \Gamma
            be the formula
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⅋
                    %mi …
                    %mo ⅋
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_1 \parr \ldots \parr A_n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo :
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi: \Gamma \to \Delta
            determines (and is determined by) a unique proof structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo :
                  %mo ⨂
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo →
                  %mo ⅋
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|\pi|}: \bigotimes \Gamma \to \parr \Delta
            with the same underlying KM-graph as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
          %p
            Then, more generally we may define a proof net of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \to \Delta
            to be a proof structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo :
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi: \Gamma \to \Delta
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|\pi|}
            is a proof net according to the definition above.
          %p Alternatively, we may define proof nets by reference to MLL sequent calculus, as follows.
          %h3#NetDeductions Nets of sequent deductions
          %p
            To each deduction or derivation tree in MLL sequent calculus, one may give an associated proof net. This is by induction; one considers the last step of a deduction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            whose premises have given derivations
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_i: \Gamma_i \to \Delta_i
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(\delta_i)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
          %p
            We recall the rules for MLL over a list of variable types
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{T}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            of a given sequent
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo →
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \to \Delta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(\delta)
            is determined up to its KM-links, and so we will give just the rules for computing the KM-graph.
          %p The rules for forming KM-graphs will sound pretty repetitive! But don’t worry; that just means they’re really easy. In summary, all we do for each rule besides the axioms is take the disjoint union of the KM-graphs occurring for the (derivations of the) premises, to get the KM-graph of the conclusion.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              By the
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cut+rule"} Hauptsatz
          %ol
            %li
              %p Axiom
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                        %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                          %mi t
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi t
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi id
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{}{\displaystyle t \vdash t}\; id,
              %p
                where
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo ∈
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \in \mathbf{T}
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi t
                          %mo −
                        %mo →
                        %msup
                          %mi t
                          %mo +
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t^- \to t^+
            %li
              %p Structural rule
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                          %mi δ
                          %mo ′
                          %mo :
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi B
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi Σ
                        %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi B
                          %mo ,
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi Σ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi exchange
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\displaystyle \delta': \Gamma, A, B, \Delta \vdash \Sigma}{\displaystyle \Gamma, B, A, \Delta \vdash \Sigma}\; exchange
              %p
                Here there is an identification between the sets of subformula occurrences for the premise and conclusion, and under this identification the proof net for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
                is the same as the proof net for
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta'
            %li
              %p Unit rules (logical rules for units). There are four of these:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                        %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mn 1
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mn 1
                        %mo +
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{}{\displaystyle \; \vdash \mathbf{1}}\; \mathbf{1}_+
              %p
                For
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mn 1
                          %mo +
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}_+
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                        %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                          %mo ⊥
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{}{\displaystyle \bot \vdash \; }\; \bot_{-}
              %p
                For
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ⊥
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot_{-}
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                          %mi δ
                          %mo ′
                          %mo :
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi Σ
                        %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ,
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi Σ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\displaystyle \delta': \Gamma, \Delta \vdash \Sigma}{\displaystyle \Gamma, \mathbf{1}, \Delta \vdash \Sigma}\; \mathbf{1}_{-}
              %p
                For
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}_{-}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
                is the same as for
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta'
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                          %mi δ
                          %mo ′
                          %mo :
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Σ
                        %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo ,
                          %mo ⊥
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Σ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mo +
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\displaystyle \delta': \Gamma \vdash \Delta, \Sigma}{\displaystyle \Gamma \vdash \Delta, \bot, \Sigma}\; \bot_+
              %p
                For
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ⊥
                          %mo +
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot_+
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
                is the same as for
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
