          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong unit-extended proof net
              is a unit-extended proof structure that is associated with some MLL sequent deduction.
          %p The notion of network for unit-extended proof structures is the same as for ordinary proof structures: a network is obtained by removing just one edge from each par-switch. The Danos-Regnier criterion for validity goes through without modification:
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p A unit-extended proof structure is a unit-extended proof net if and only if each of its networks is an acyclic connected graph (ignoring edge orientations).
          %p
            The simplified algorithm for deciding validity, in terms of a series of
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#DR"} graph reductions
          %h2#comparison_to_string_diagrams Comparison to string diagrams
          %p
            There are various possibilities for translating the language of proof nets into a language of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
          %p (I’ll come back to this. I want to think some more on how I want to say it.)
